#
# Semantic content of the header page: values, relations and properties.
# The presence of each name and type comes with the Tier 0 generator.

assertion  XBD.stdint.h.001
anchor     basedefs/stdint.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       int8_t, int16_t and int32_t (and int64_t when a 64-bit type exists)
           are signed types of exactly 8, 16, 32 and 64 bits, and uint8_t to
           uint64_t unsigned ones of the same widths; a standard integer type of
           one of those widths has a matching exact-width type.
note       Only the widths 8, 16, 32 and 64 are examined.
test       t/001-exact-types.c
nomutant   compile-time check of header content; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.stdint.h.002
anchor     basedefs/stdint.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       INTN_MIN is exactly -2^(N-1), INTN_MAX exactly 2^(N-1)-1 and UINTN_MAX
           exactly 2^N-1 for the exact-width types, usable in #if, each with the
           type an object of the matching type has after the integer promotions.
test       t/002-exact-limits.c
nomutant   compile-time check of header content; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.stdint.h.003
anchor     basedefs/stdint.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       int_leastN_t and uint_leastN_t exist for N of 8, 16, 32 and 64, are
           signed and unsigned, are at least N bits wide, are the smallest such
           types (no standard type with a smaller range is wide enough), and
           their limit macros reach the stated magnitudes and have the promoted
           type.
note       Only the widths 8, 16, 32 and 64 are examined.
test       t/003-least-types.c
nomutant   compile-time check of header content; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.stdint.h.004
anchor     basedefs/stdint.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       int_fastN_t and uint_fastN_t exist for N of 8, 16, 32 and 64, are
           signed and unsigned, are at least N bits wide, and their limit macros
           reach the stated magnitudes and have the promoted type.
note       Only the widths 8, 16, 32 and 64 are examined.
test       t/004-fast-types.c
nomutant   compile-time check of header content; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.stdint.h.005
anchor     basedefs/stdint.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       When intptr_t and uintptr_t are provided, a valid void pointer
           converted to either and back compares equal to the original, intptr_t
           is signed and uintptr_t unsigned, and their limits reach -32768, 32767
           and 65535 in magnitude with the promoted type.
note       These types are optional unless the system is XSI; a system that
           provides neither gives NOTINUSE.
test       t/005-pointer-types.c
nomutant   compile-time check of header content; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.stdint.h.006
anchor     basedefs/stdint.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       On an XSI system intptr_t and uintptr_t exist, and INTPTR_MIN,
           INTPTR_MAX and UINTPTR_MAX are defined.
test       t/006-pointer-types-xsi.c
nomutant   compile-time check of header content; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.stdint.h.007
anchor     basedefs/stdint.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       intmax_t is a signed type and uintmax_t an unsigned type that hold
           every value of every standard signed and unsigned type; intmax_t is
           two's complement; INTMAX_MIN, INTMAX_MAX and UINTMAX_MAX reach -2^63,
           2^63-1 and 2^64-1 in magnitude, with the promoted type.
test       t/007-greatest-width.c
nomutant   compile-time check of header content; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.stdint.h.008
anchor     basedefs/stdint.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       PTRDIFF_MIN, PTRDIFF_MAX and SIZE_MAX reach -65536, 65535 and 65535 in
           magnitude; SIG_ATOMIC, WCHAR and WINT limits follow the signedness of
           their types (signed: no greater than -127, -127 and -32767 and no less
           than 127, 127 and 32767; unsigned: 0 and at least 255, 255 and 65535);
           all have the promoted type of the matching type.
test       t/008-other-limits.c
nomutant   compile-time check of header content; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.stdint.h.009
anchor     basedefs/stdint.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       INTN_C and UINTN_C (N of 8, 16, 32, 64) expand to integer constant
           expressions usable in #if with the argument's value and the promoted
           type of int_leastN_t and uint_leastN_t; INTMAX_C and UINTMAX_C do the
           same with the types intmax_t and uintmax_t.
note       Only the widths 8, 16, 32 and 64 are examined.
test       t/009-constant-macros.c
nomutant   compile-time check of header content; needs the Tier 0 header mutants

assertion  XBD.stdint.h.010
anchor     basedefs/stdint.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A type named with and without the leading u (exact, least, fast,
           pointer, greatest width) is provided together with its counterpart,
           the two have the same size, and the MIN, MAX and UMAX limit macros of
           a type are defined together.
note       Only the widths 8, 16, 32 and 64 are examined.
test       t/010-signed-unsigned-pairs.c
nomutant   compile-time check of header content; needs the Tier 0 header mutants
